  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM MDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery shows clear skies across eastern \nIdaho under weak high-pressure control. Temperatures will top \nout in the upper 70s to near 80\u00b0F late this afternoon before \nskies remain clear overnight. Pattern changes begin Thursday as \nan approaching Pacific Northwest upper-level trough tightens the\nregional pressure gradient. Winds will increase markedly on \nThursday alongside a warming trend that will push valley highs \ninto the 80s. Breezy-to-windy conditions will persist through \nFriday. Slightly lower 500mb heights on Friday will bring a \nsubtle temperature dip, while shortwave energy rotating out of \nBritish Columbia aids in consolidating a more potent upper-level\nlow to our northwest. Aside from a low-end shower chance on \nFriday, conditions will remain predominantly dry through late \nSaturday afternoon.\n\nThe incoming weekend low will drive a sharp temperature roller \ncoaster across eastern Idaho. Strong southerly flow ahead of the\ntrough axis will pump unseasonable heat back into the region on\nSaturday, pushing lower-elevation highs into the mid-80s. A \nsharp cold front will cross the area Saturday night into Sunday,\nbringing a dramatic 15 to 20-degree temperature drop. Highs on \nSunday will struggle to escape the 50s and 60s amidst continued \ngusty winds. Precipitation will overspread the region Saturday \nnight into Sunday, with atmospheric moisture focused primarily \nacross northern zones, including the Central Mountains, \nContinental Divide, and Island Park.\n\nIn the wake of the exiting low, precipitation will wrap up early\nMonday, leaving seasonably cool and dry conditions to start the\nworkweek. Global models show high-pressure ridging overspreading\nthe Intermountain West Tuesday into Wednesday, initiating a\nsteady warming trend and ensuring dry weather through mid-week.",
